starting from judiciously selected template frameworks, PCN$333({\mathsf{S c}})^{15}.$ , MIL-100(Sc)17, MOF-74 $(Z\mathsf{n})^{18}$ , and MOF-74 $({\mathsf{M g}})^{19}$ , we successfully synthesized a series of porous photoactive titanium MOFs, PCN-333(Sc)-Ti, MIL-100(Sc)-Ti, MOF-74(Zn)-Ti and MOF-74(Mg)-Ti, which is accomplished through Sc(III) to Ti(III) or $Z\mathsf{n}(||)/\mathsf{M g}(||)$ to Ti(III) metathesis first, followed by a mild and effective oxidation step to Ti(IV) (Fig. 1). Generally, high valence metathesis is difficult and rare, thus, its application in MOF preparation represents a significant step forward in Ti-MOFs synthesis. The Ti-MOFs made via HVMO not only maintain their crystallinity throughout the whole synthetic process, but also demonstrate excellent photocatalytic property.
## Results and discussion
Postsynthetic metathesis emerged as an alternative synthetic strategy due to its exceptional capability of obtaining certain MOFs that cannot or hardly be achieved directly.20 S. Sevov and coworkers have initially demonstrated the successful use of oxidizing Ti(III) centers to obtain Ti(IV) open frameworks.21 Recently, the feasibility of postsynthetic exchange of Ti(IV) into MOFs has been demonstrated by Cohen22 and Dincǎ’s groups.23 Moreover, HVMO strategy is built upon our previously reported post-synthetic metathesis and oxidation (PSMO) strategy.24 In the metathesis step of our strategy, starting from the divalent metal species, it is surprising to see that the trivalent metal species were successfully exchanged into the template MOFs, which is not possible as demonstrated by PSMO method. More importantly, we would be able to get the trivalent metal species intermediate MOFs even starting from the supposedly stable trivalent metal based template MOFs. The prerequisites to successfully utilize the HVMO strategy are carefully examined, both for the targeting metal species and for the template MOFs.
The prerequisites for the targeting metal specViews A(rtTicil(eIVO)nlianes an example) in the HVMO approach incluDdOeI: 1fi0r.1st0,3t9/hCe5tSaCr0g3e6t2i0nHg high valent metal species have to exhibit a suitable reduced state that can be conveniently achieved. It should be pointed out that during the synthesis of the Ti-MOFs, a pure titanium(III) source with good stability under exchange environment is crucial. In our case, the commercially available, titanium(III) chloride tetrahydrofuran complex (1:3), ${\mathsf{T i C l}}_{3}{\left({\mathsf{T H F}}\right)}_{3},$ was utilized as the titanium(III) source. Second, the reduced species in lower oxidation state can undergo metal metathesis with a considerable rate to guarantee the framework crystallinity. The reduced state Ti(III), with lower charge and larger radius, undergoes a ligand dissociation rate around $10^{5}~{\mathsf{s e c}}^{-1}$ in the aqueous media, which is even faster than some M(II) transition metal species in the third period, such as Ni(II).25 Third, the reduced species can survive under the exchanging environment. Ti(III)/Ti(IV) has higher redox potential than $\mathsf{C r}(||)/\mathsf{C r}(|||)$ in similar coordination environment of weak field ligands, making Ti(III) easier to handle than Cr(II), which is used in PSMO method, during the exchange process. Ti(IV) meets all these prerequisites, which enables HVMO strategy promising to synthesize Ti-MOFs.
Meanwhile, the template MOFs were also selected with careful consideration. Firstly, the metal species in the template MOFs cannot be reduced by Ti(III). Otherwise, Ti(III) would be oxidized to strong Lewis acidic Ti(IV) while the metals in the template MOFs would be reduced to lower valent metal ions. The lower valent metal based MOFs would be easily damaged by strong Lewis acidic Ti(IV) species. To verify this necessity, we conducted metathesis with PCN-333(Fe), which totally dissolved in one minute after we added Ti(III) species (ESI, Section 4). Secondly, all the template MOFs should contain open metal sites, which are occupied by the weakly coordinated neutral solvent molecules. These open metal sites could effectively accelerate the metathesis rate. Thirdly, the metal species in the template should possess similar coordination environment with targeting metal species. In this case, they are all six-coordinated. Last but not least, metal−ligand bonds in template MOFs should have certain lability to drive the metal exchange to complete. Otherwise, the exchange time would be very long and the crystallinity will be decreased during this process. With all these considerations in mind, we have chosen PCN-333(Sc), MIL-100(Sc), MOF$74(2\mathsf{n})$ , and MOF-74(Mg) as our template MOFs.
It is believed that HVMO strategy demonstrated in this study greatly facilitates the development of the original PSMO method as a general yet powerful strategy for synthesizing novel MOFs which cannot be obtained using the conventional method. And as expected, a series of highly porous Ti-MOFs were obtained using this HVMO strategy, while the direct synthesis using Ti(III) and the corresponding linkers under solvothermal and anaerobic conditions only resulted in amorphous powders. The typical synthetic process of PCN333(Sc)-Ti is: First, after washed with anhydrous N,N
